<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Meinhard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1134–1196</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Meinhard of Segeberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meinhard was an Augustinian canon from Segeberg in Holstein who began the Christian mission among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a>. He built a church and a stone castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikskile">Üxküll</a> and was consecrated the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> in 1186. Venerated as a saint, he is regarded as the founder of the Church in Latvia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-meinhard.jpg" alt="Saint Meinhard" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Latvia United at Latvian Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ik%C5%A1%C4%B7iles_bazn%C4%ABcas_drupas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As a canon of Segeberg Abbey in Holstein, Meinhard was possibly inspired by the missionary work of Vicelinus among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. In the 1170s or early 1180s he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> with merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, who were probably trading in costly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, to convert the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a>. He settled on the Daugava River at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikskile">Ikšķile</a>, in German Üxküll, southeast of present-day Riga, and in 1184 built a stone church there dedicated to Our Lady.</p><p>After an attack by Lithuanian raiders looking to carry off slaves, Meinhard brought stonemasons from Gotland to build a fortress. These were the first known stone buildings among the Baltic tribes. A second stone castle at Salaspils, in German Holm, was a gift to newly converted pagans, but its people rebelled and tried to drive him out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. Meinhard first sought converts by peaceful means; faced with resistance and apostasy, he turned to the idea of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>.</p><p>On a brief return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1186 he was consecrated by Hartwig of Uthlede, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>. Pope Clement III confirmed the new bishopric in September 1188 and in 1190 allowed any monk to join the mission. In April 1193 Pope Celestine III authorised active recruitment, relaxed the rules on monks' food and clothing and granted indulgences to those who joined. Among the recruits was Theodorich of Loccum Abbey, who began a mission at Turaida, in German Treyden.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Meinhard died in 1196, on either 14 August or 11 October, and his life is told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-chronicle-of-henry">Livonian Chronicle of Henry</a>. His successors, Berthold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Albert of Riga</a>, began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusade</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>, a crusading military order, in Riga. His remains were moved to Riga in 1226 and rest in Riga Cathedral, now Lutheran.</p><p>On 8 September 1993 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a>, visiting the Baltic states, proclaimed that the veneration of Saint Meinhard would be restored on 14 August each year, an act considered equivalent to canonisation. Remains of his church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikskile">Ikšķile</a> survive. When the Riga Hydroelectric Power Plant was built in the 1970s, an artificial island was raised to keep the water from flooding the ruins, which were covered with metal in 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint Meinhard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The missionary <b>Meinhard</b> became bishop of the Livs at Üxküll in 1186.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikskile">Ikšķile</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.47,9.51&amp;year=1174" aria-label="Schleswig-Holstein on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4760 298 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saint Meinhard"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5210 568h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5210 568h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.47,9.51&amp;year=1174">What was here in 1174? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
